The competitive ratio measures an online algorithm (which sees the input one piece at a time) against an optimal offline algorithm that knows everything in advance. An algorithm is c-competitive if its cost is at most c times the offline optimum on every input, plus a constant.

```python
# ski rental: rent costs 1/day, buying costs 10. Buy after 10 rental days.
def online_cost(days):
    return days if days <= 10 else 20  # 10 renting + 10 buying

def offline_cost(days):
    return min(days, 10)

print(online_cost(100) / offline_cost(100))  # 2.0
```

This classic strategy is 2-competitive.

A Bloom filter is a compact probabilistic set. It can say "definitely not present" or "probably present": false positives are possible, false negatives are not. It uses a bit array and several hash functions.

```python
import hashlib

class Bloom:
    def __init__(self, size=1000, k=3):
        self.bits = bytearray(size)
        self.size, self.k = size, k
    def _idx(self, item):
        for i in range(self.k):
            h = hashlib.sha256(f"{i}{item}".encode()).digest()
            yield int.from_bytes(h[:4], "big") % self.size
    def add(self, item):
        for i in self._idx(item):
            self.bits[i] = 1
    def __contains__(self, item):
        return all(self.bits[i] for i in self._idx(item))

b = Bloom()
b.add("cat")
print("cat" in b, "dog" in b)  # True False
```

You can't delete items from a plain Bloom filter.

